---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import claims from '../data/claims.json'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const gh = 'https://github.com/joshedwards237/Amanuensis'
const head = (claims.bands as Record<string, any>)[claims.headline_band]

const toc = [
  ['requirements', 'Requirements'],
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['permissions', 'Permissions'],
  ['first-dictation', 'Your first dictation'],
  ['config', 'Configuration'],
  ['vocabulary', 'Custom vocabulary'],
  ['history', 'History and retention'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Base
  current="docs"
  title="Install Amanuensis — documentation"
  description="Install, permissions, configuration and troubleshooting for Amanuensis, local dictation for macOS."
>
  <div class="docs page">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="t-label">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        {toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <article class="body">
      <p class="t-label">Documentation</p>
      <h1 class="t-h2">Install Amanuensis</h1>
      <p class="t-lede">
        About two minutes, most of it waiting for a model to download. macOS only.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Runs from a source checkout.</strong> There is no packaged app, installer or
        signed binary yet — that is Phase 4. If you want a double-clickable <code>.app</code>, watch <a href={`${gh}/releases`}>releases</a> rather than installing today.
      </div>

      <h2 id="requirements" class="t-h3">Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>macOS.</strong> The hotkey, the injection path and the menu-bar indicator are all platform-specific.</li>
        <li><strong>Python 3.12 or newer.</strong> <code>python3 --version</code> to check.</li>
        <li><strong>A desk microphone.</strong> Published figures were measured on one; a Bluetooth headset has a different acoustic profile.</li>
        <li><strong>About 100 MB of disk</strong> for the model, downloaded once.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="install" class="t-h3">Install</h2>
      <pre><code>git clone {gh}
cd Amanuensis
python3 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .</code></pre>

      <p>Then download the model and measure this machine:</p>
      <pre><code>manu install</code></pre>

      <p class="t-caption">
        <code>manu install</code> fetches the speech model once over HTTPS from a pinned
        revision and checksum-verifies it. It then times a reference clip to work out which
        tier your machine is in, and writes that to disk. Nothing is downloaded at runtime
        ever again.
      </p>

      <h2 id="permissions" class="t-h3">Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        macOS will ask for two. The daemon names whichever is missing and refuses to start
        rather than half-working:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> — to notice the hotkey. The event tap is
          listen-only; it does not modify or swallow your keystrokes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessibility</strong> — to place text at the cursor in another
          application.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="t-caption">
        System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → the relevant list → add your terminal (or
        the Python binary). You will need to restart the daemon after granting.
      </p>

      <h2 id="first-dictation" class="t-h3">Your first dictation</h2>
      <pre><code>manu daemon</code></pre>
      <p>
        Leave it running. Put your cursor anywhere you can type, hold <kbd>right ⌥</kbd>,
        say a sentence, and let go. The text appears where your cursor is.
      </p>
      <p>The menu-bar glyph tells you the state:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Glyph</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="g">○</td><td>Idle. The microphone is held but not recording.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="g rec">●</td><td>Recording. Your voice is being captured.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="g">◐</td><td>Transcribing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="g">◍</td><td>Recovered — the transcript was re-decoded without your vocabulary bias, so proper nouns are less reliable.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="g rec">⚠</td><td>Withheld. The words were <em>not</em> injected; <code>manu history --last</code> has them.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-caption">
        For a ten-second utterance, expect text about {head.p50_ms} ms after you release the
        key (p95 {head.p95_ms} ms, measured over {head.n} real dictations). Longer dictations
        take proportionally longer — <a href={`${base}/how-it-works#transcribe`}>the measured
        bands are here</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="config" class="t-h3">Configuration</h2>
      <p>
        Everything that could reasonably go either way is a key in
        <code>~/.config/amanuensis/config.toml</code>. The file is created with defaults on
        first run.
      </p>
      <pre><code>[hotkey]
key = "right_option"

[injection]
strategy = "clipboard"   # or "keystroke"

[history]
retain = true
retain_days = 30</code></pre>
      <div class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>A real trade, stated plainly.</strong> <code>clipboard</code> passes your
        transcript through the system clipboard, so a clipboard manager will capture it —
        and one that syncs across devices will move it off this machine.
        <code>keystroke</code> avoids the clipboard, but macOS text substitution silently
        rewrites synthetic keystrokes (five changes in one measured sentence). The private
        strategy is the one that alters your words. <a href={`${base}/how-it-works#inject`}
          >The measurements are here</a
        >.
      </div>

      <h2 id="vocabulary" class="t-h3">Custom vocabulary</h2>
      <p>
        Names, jargon and acronyms the model will not know. Edit
        <code>~/.config/amanuensis/vocabulary.toml</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>[replace]
"kubernetes" = "Kubernetes"

[boost]
terms = ["Amanuensis", "faster-whisper"]</code></pre>
      <p>Check it parses and see what it would do:</p>
      <pre><code>manu vocab check</code></pre>

      <h2 id="history" class="t-h3">History and retention</h2>
      <pre><code>manu history --last      # the most recent transcript
manu history --pending   # transcripts that were never injected
manu history --raw       # before the cleanup pass
manu history --purge     # delete beyond retain_days</code></pre>
      <p class="t-caption">
        Setting <code>retain = false</code> does not skip the write — it writes to a
        mode-0600 temporary file that never enters the database and is deleted the moment
        injection succeeds. "Nothing persists" and "a crash never loses a transcript" are
        both true at once, and the write itself is deliberately not user-settable.
      </p>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting" class="t-h3">Troubleshooting</h2>

      <h3 class="t-h3 sub">The daemon will not start</h3>
      <p>It names the missing permission. Grant it, then restart the daemon — the permission is read at startup.</p>

      <h3 class="t-h3 sub">Nothing happens when I hold the key</h3>
      <p>
        Check Input Monitoring. If the glyph never turns <span class="g rec">●</span>, the
        event tap is not seeing the key.
      </p>

      <h3 class="t-h3 sub">I got ⚠ and no text</h3>
      <p>
        That is the collapse guard refusing to inject a transcript it judged broken.
        <code>manu history --last</code> has your words. It fires when the decoder got
        through too little of your speech.
      </p>

      <h3 class="t-h3 sub">My clipboard keeps getting replaced</h3>
      <p>
        Expected with <code>strategy = "clipboard"</code>. It saves and restores, but the
        restore races with clipboard managers. Switch to <code>keystroke</code> if that
        matters more to you than exact text.
      </p>

      <h3 class="t-h3 sub">It is slower than the numbers say</h3>
      <p>
        The published figures are Tier A on one machine. <code>manu install</code> recorded
        your tier; slower hardware is genuinely slower, and Tier B has never been measured
        against its own published bar.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        Still stuck? <a href={`${gh}/issues`}>Open an issue</a>. Include the output of
        <code>manu install</code>, which reports your tier.
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .docs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 14rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s5);
    padding: var(--s5) var(--s3) var(--s6);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .toc { position: sticky; top: 5rem; }
  .toc ul { list-style: none; margin: 0.75rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .toc li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .toc a { color: var(--ink-2); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--iris); }
  .body { max-width: 42rem; }
  .body h1 { margin: 0.35rem 0 var(--s2); }
  .body h2 { margin: var(--s5) 0 var(--s2); scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .body h3.sub { font-size: 1.0625rem; margin: var(--s3) 0 0.35rem; }
  .body p, .body ul, .body table { margin-block: var(--s2); }
  .body pre { margin-block: var(--s2); }
  .body ul { padding-left: 1.15rem; }
  .body li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .g { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .g.rec { color: var(--accent); }
  .callout {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--iris);
    background: var(--iris-soft);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: var(--s2) var(--s3);
    margin-block: var(--s3);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .callout-warn {
    border-left-color: var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .docs { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s3); }
    .toc { position: static; }
  }
</style>
